+## JSON Patch (RFC 6902)
+
+```ts
+import { applyPatch, JsonPatchError } from "./src/index.ts";
+
+applyPatch({ foo: ["bar"] }, [
+  { op: "add", path: "/foo/-", value: "baz" },
+  { op: "test", path: "/foo/0", value: "bar" },
+  { op: "move", from: "/foo", path: "/list" },
+]); // { list: ["bar", "baz"] }
+```
+
+- `applyPatch(document, patch)` applies the operations of `patch` (`add`, `remove`, `replace`, `move`,
+  `copy`, `test`) in order and returns the resulting document as a new value. It never modifies
+  `document` or `patch`, and the result shares no object with them.
+- It throws a `JsonPatchError` when the patch is invalid or an operation fails; nothing is returned,
+  so a patch applies entirely or not at all. The error carries the failing operation's `index` and
+  `operation`, and its `cause` is the `JsonPointerError` when a pointer is at fault. A patch is invalid
+  when it is not an array, or an operation is not an object, has an unknown `op`, or lacks a string
+  `path`, a string `from` (`move`, `copy`) or a `value` (`add`, `replace`, `test`). An operation fails
+  when a location it reads or removes does not exist, an `add` targets a missing parent or an array
+  index above the length, a `move` targets one of its own children, a `test` finds a different value,
+  or the document or a `value` is not a JSON value (`undefined`, a function, `NaN`, a `Date`...).
+- Members other than `op`, `path`, `from` and `value` are ignored. `-` names the end of an array only
+  as the target of `add`, `move` or `copy`; removing the whole document (`remove` with path `""`) is
+  an error.
+- `test` compares JSON values: same type, numbers by value, object members in any order, array
+  elements in order.
+- Pointers follow the JSON Pointer rules above; a member named `__proto__` is added as an ordinary
+  member and never changes a prototype.
+
 Run the tests with `npm test` (Node.js 22.18 or later, which runs TypeScript directly).
