+---
+id: dl-003-json-patch-atomic-copy-on-apply-semantics-and-error-reporting
+type: decision-log
+title: "JSON Patch: atomic copy-on-apply semantics and error reporting"
+status: pending
+format: 1
+---
+
+# JSON Patch: atomic copy-on-apply semantics and error reporting
+
+## Context
+
+`applyPatch(document, patch)` (task-002) implements RFC 6902 and must not mutate its input. The RFC
+leaves some points open or implicit: what happens to the document when an operation fails mid-patch
+(section 5), whether the whole document may be removed, how the result of `test` compares values,
+and how values that are not JSON (JavaScript `undefined`, `Date`, `NaN`) are handled. dl-001 and
+dl-002 already fix the pointer semantics and expose `parsePointer`/`JsonPointerError` for reuse.
+
+## Decision
+
+- Copy-on-apply: the document is deep-copied once, every operation is applied to the copy, and each
+  inserted `value` (and each `copy` source) is deep-copied. The result shares no object with the inputs.
+- Atomic: the first failing operation stops evaluation and throws; no partial result is returned.
+- Every failure throws a `JsonPatchError` (exported) carrying the failing operation's `index` and the
+  `operation` itself; when a pointer is at fault, its `cause` is the `JsonPointerError`.
+- Validation is lazy, operation by operation, as the RFC evaluates them in order; since nothing is
+  returned on failure, this is indistinguishable from validating up front.
+- Pointer resolution reuses `src/json-pointer.ts` (moved out of `src/index.ts`, which now only
+  re-exports), including dl-001's strictness: `-` is accepted only as the last token of an `add`
+  target (so also of `move`/`copy` targets); `remove`, `replace`, `test` and `from` reject it.
+- `remove` of the whole document (`""`) is an error: a patch must leave a JSON document.
+- `move` onto its own location is a no-op (its source must still exist); onto one of its children,
+  compared by decoded tokens, is an error.
+- `test` uses JSON equality (section 4.6): same type, numbers by value, members in any order.
+- Document and values must be JSON values (plain objects, arrays, strings, finite numbers, booleans,
+  null); anything else throws a `JsonPatchError`.
+- Members are written as own data properties, so `/__proto__` is an ordinary member.
+- Unknown operation members are ignored (section 4).
+
+## Consequences
+
+- Each call costs a deep copy of the document, even for a patch that only tests: simple and safe,
+  at O(size of document) per call.
+- Callers get all-or-nothing behaviour and can locate the failing operation through `index`.
+- Patches from untrusted sources cannot pollute prototypes or read inherited properties.
+- `JsonPatchError` (with `index`, `operation`) is now public API and must stay stable.
